{"candidates":[{"claim":"Idempotent and safe are distinct: safe means read-only (no server state change), while idempotent means state may change but identical repeated requests yield the same result as a single one. An append-only event log surviving at-least-once delivery requires writers to be idempotent, not safe, because writers must modify state yet retries must not compound.","mechanism":"At-least-once delivery retries writes that may have already succeeded. If the write were merely safe it could not record an event at all (safe = no state mutation). If the write were non-idempotent, each retry would append a duplicate event, corrupting replay determinism. The causal mechanism is deduplication: the writer attaches a stable operation key (or sequence number) so the log's append path can detect a replayed entry and discard it, collapsing the effect of N retries to the effect of one. This transforms a raw append (neither safe nor idempotent) into an effectively idempotent operation. RFC 9110 separates the two precisely along this axis: safe methods are cacheable/prefetchable because they have no side effects; idempotent methods (PUT, DELETE) have side effects but are repeatable.","counterconditions":["If RFC 9110 defines 'safe' to include idempotent methods by inclusion (safe ⊂ idempotent), the strict separation breaks.","If the log architecture uses single-writer (single-leader) with no retry, idempotency may be unnecessary.","If at-least-once delivery is upgraded to exactly-once via broker-side transactional commits, the writer need not itself be idempotent."],"optional_refs":["SRC_001"],"typicality":0.92},{"claim":"Safe is a strictly stronger property than idempotent in the sense that all safe methods are idempotent (repeating a no-op yields a no-op), but the converse fails. An append-only event log needs the weaker property — idempotent writers — because it must tolerate side effects (writing events) while bounding them under retry.","mechanism":"RFC 9110 classifies safe methods as idempotent because a read-only operation repeated produces no additional state change. The hierarchy is: safe ⇒ idempotent, but idempotent ⇏ safe (PUT is idempotent but not safe). The log's writers occupy the idempotent-but-not-safe cell: they mutate the log, but their mutation is repeatable. The mechanism is conditional append keyed by a content hash or client-side idempotency token; the log stores a fingerprint of prior writes and rejects duplicates at the append boundary. This is idempotence engineered into an unsafe (state-mutating) operation.","counterconditions":["If RFC 9110 does not formally state that safe methods are a subset of idempotent methods, the hierarchy claim fails.","If the log uses a monotonically increasing sequence number that makes raw append naturally idempotent (impossible duplicates by construction), the distinction collapses."],"optional_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003"],"typicality":0.82},{"claim":"The RFC 9110 distinction is HTTP-semantics-local and does not transfer cleanly to event-log writer semantics. The append-only log does not require 'safe' or 'idempotent' in the RFC sense at all; it requires a stronger, log-specific property: deterministic-replay idempotence, where the replay of a sequence of operations reproduces the same final state regardless of delivery multiplicity, which is neither pure safety nor pure idempotence.","mechanism":"RFC 9110 defines idempotence per-method: 'several identical requests have the same effect as one.' But an event log operates on sequences with causality (ordering, dependencies). The required property is that the composition of retried appends is equivalent to a single canonical sequence — a property of the aggregate, not of individual operations. The mechanism is content-addressing or operation-ID deduplication over the entire write history, which subsumes per-operation idempotence. Calling it 'idempotent' (RFC sense) is a useful approximation but technically conflates per-method semantics with sequence semantics.","counterconditions":["If RFC 9110's definition of idempotence is broad enough to cover sequences (it is per-method), this candidate is wrong and the standard view holds.","If the log's correctness proof only needs per-operation idempotence (not sequence-level), the stronger property is unnecessary."],"optional_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_003"],"typicality":0.35},{"claim":"The append-only event log requires safe operations from its readers and idempotent operations from its writers: the asymmetry is structural. Writers mutate (so not safe) but must be repeatable (idempotent); readers must not mutate (safe) and therefore are trivially idempotent.","mechanism":"At-least-once delivery applies to both read and write paths in distributed logs. On the write path, idempotence via deduplication keys is the minimal requirement because safety would forbid writes. On the read path, safety suffices and implies idempotence for free. The causal mechanism is the split of the log protocol into a mutation channel (writer→log, idempotent) and an observation channel (reader←log, safe). This maps to RFC 9110's separation of methods into those that change state (PUT, POST, DELETE) and those that don't (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS).","counterconditions":["If at-least-once delivery only affects the write path (not reads), the reader-side safety claim is vacuous.","If the log permits readers to trigger side effects (e.g., consumer-offset commits), readers are not safe either."],"optional_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_004"],"typicality":0.65}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":["semantic","focus"],"purpose":"Verify exact definitions of 'safe' and 'idempotent' in RFC 9110 to ground the distinction and the subset relationship, and to quote the authoritative text.","query":"RFC 9110 definition of safe methods and idempotent methods, exact wording","requested_visible_aliases":["SRC_001","SRC_003"]},"research_proposals":[{"purpose":"Fetch the exact definitions of 'safe' and 'idempotent' from RFC 9110 to cite verbatim. The target section defines safe methods as read-only and idempotent methods as repeatable, and states whether safe methods are a subset of idempotent methods.","request_identifier":"rfc9110-method-definitions","urls":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-9.2","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-9.2.1","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-9.2.2"]}],"scratch_proposal":{"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Open question: Does RFC 9110 explicitly state safe ⊂ idempotent, or is the subset relationship only implied? Need verbatim quote from the RFC to confirm the hierarchy candidate.","why_keep_this":"This unresolved question directly determines whether candidate #2's hierarchy claim survives or must be weakened.","unfinished":"Waiting on research fetch of RFC 9110 §9.2."},"local_key":"NEW_001"},{"body":{"content":"Counterfactual: If the event log used a single-writer, single-threaded, no-retry model, idempotence would be unnecessary. But this breaks the at-least-once delivery assumption — the problem explicitly states retries happen. So the necessity of idempotence is conditional on the delivery model, not on the log itself.","why_keep_this":"Clarifies that the required property is jointly determined by delivery semantics and operation semantics, not by the log alone.","unfinished":"Could refine to show exactly how at-least-once delivery forces the idempotence requirement."},"local_key":"NEW_002"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Does RFC 9110 define idempotence over the observable response, over server state, or both? This matters: if idempotence is defined over responses only, the event-log deduplication requirement is a separate, stronger property.","related_refs":["NEW_001"]}],"links":[{"from_ref":"NEW_001","relation_hint":"NEW_001's resolution determines whether candidate #2's subset hierarchy is valid","to_ref":"NEW_002"}]}}